                    <div class="kb-article-body">
                        <h1>From Checkout to Refund: Mapping the D2C Profit Journey</h1>
                        <p>
                            A footwear brand in Bengaluru reconstructed one order last quarter. Value ₹2,400. Prepaid. Delivered on time. No complaints logged.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The customer exchanged it once for a half-size up. The reverse pickup failed twice. The replacement shipped from a warehouse 900km away because the source warehouse was out of that size. Support touched the ticket four times. By the time the order closed, contribution margin was <strong>negative ₹180</strong>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Nobody saw it. The checkout dashboard recorded a conversion. The logistics dashboard recorded a delivery. The returns tool recorded an exchange. Three systems, three green lights, one loss.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            That is what a <strong>checkout to refund journey</strong> looks like when you stop measuring it in stages and start measuring it as one financial event. Profit mapping across the post-order funnel is not a reporting exercise. It is the only way to know which orders you should be running more of, and which ones you are quietly subsidising.
                        </p>

                        <!-- Section 1 -->
                        <h2 id="item-1">Why the Order Is the Wrong Unit and the Journey Is the Right One</h2>
                        <p>
                            Most Indian D2C brands measure profitability at SKU or campaign level. Both are reasonable. Neither explains why two identical orders for the same SKU, from the same ad set, end up with wildly different economics.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The divergence happens after checkout—in payment method, pincode, courier assignment, delivery attempt count, and whether the customer initiates a return, an exchange, or nothing at all. The order is where money enters. The journey is where it leaves.
                        </p>

                        <h3>The 5 Decisions That Determine an Order’s Economics</h3>
                        <p>
                            Each decision is measured somewhere in your stack. Almost none is measured against the others. A profit map connects all five so an early choice remains visible later.
                        </p>

                        <h4><strong>Payment Mode and Courier Assignment</strong></h4>
                        <p>
                            Payment mode sets the first risk profile, while courier assignment determines how that risk is handled in fulfilment. Treating them as separate decisions hides their combined effect.
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>COD Risk Begins at Checkout:</strong> COD carries an RTO probability several times higher than prepaid. That probability is a cost, whether or not it appears on an invoice.</li>
                            <li><strong>Courier Choice Shapes the Outcome:</strong> The cheapest partner for a Tier 3 pincode is frequently the one with the worst first-attempt success rate on that lane.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4><strong>Warehouse, Delivery, and Return Decisions</strong></h4>
                        <p>
                            Warehouse selection determines whether a split shipment or distant source adds forward cost. Delivery outcome determines whether the order succeeds in one attempt or three. Return type determines whether revenue disappears through a refund or survives through an exchange that brings additional logistics cost. The right outcome depends on the SKU, lane, and margin—not on a universal rule.
                        </p>

                        <!-- Section 2 -->
                        <h2 id="item-2">Where Profit Actually Leaks in the Delivery Lifecycle</h2>
                        <p>
                            There is a comfortable assumption in Indian D2C that RTO is the primary post-order cost. It is not. RTO is the most visible post-order cost, which is a different thing.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            An RTO is a discrete, unmissable event with a number attached. Brands optimise against it while ignoring costs distributed across too many line items to trigger an alarm.
                        </p>

                        <!-- Diagram 1 -->
                        <div class="diag-container">
                            <div class="kb-main-image-wrapper">
                                <img
                                    src="images/image6.png"
                                    alt="Visible and hidden costs across the order lifecycle"
                                    loading="lazy"
                                />
                            </div>
