                        </div>

                        <p>
                            In apparel and footwear, the hidden column frequently exceeds the visible one. Most brands have a return-attribution problem: they know the aggregate spend but not which orders, lanes, or SKUs generated it.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            A failed first delivery attempt in India rarely costs one reattempt fee. Its financial effect compounds across logistics, customer support, time, and downstream customer behaviour.
                        </p>

                        <h3 id="item-2-1">The Compounding Cost of Delivery Friction</h3>
                        <p>
                            The true cost includes the reattempt fee, a support interaction, the chance that the second attempt fails, and the elevated chance that a customer who waited four extra days returns the order on arrival. That last item rarely enters a spreadsheet. Treating NDR and returns as separate cost centres understates both.
                        </p>

                        <h3 id="item-2-2">Why Courier NDR Codes Make the Loss Invisible</h3>
                        <p>
                            When one partner logs “customer unavailable” and another logs “premises closed” for the same underlying failure, you cannot correlate delivery friction with downstream return rates. The data exists, but it is not comparable.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Pragma’s <a href="https://www.bepragma.ai/product/rto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="kb-body-link">RTO Suite</a> normalises NDR reasons across couriers for precisely this reason. Normalisation is not cosmetic; it is the precondition for connecting two stages that currently cannot see each other.
                        </p>

                        <!-- Diagram 2 -->
                        <div class="diag-container">
