                        <div class="kb-faq-section" id="item-8">
                            <div class="kb-faq-header">
                                <h3 class="kb-faq-title">FAQs</h3>
                                <p class="kb-faq-subtitle">
                                    (Frequently Asked Questions On From Checkout to Refund: Mapping the D2C Profit Journey)
                                </p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="kb-faq-body">
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">1. What is a checkout to refund journey and why does it matter more than tracking individual stages?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        A checkout-to-refund journey tracks the complete financial lifecycle of an order. It reveals how decisions made at checkout, dispatch, and delivery create costs that stage-level dashboards often miss.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">2. How do I calculate the true contribution margin of an order rather than the gross margin?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Start with the order value after discounts and payment fees. Subtract actual forward and reverse logistics, reattempts, support interactions, refund fees, and any loss from markdown or unsaleable inventory.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">3. Is RTO really not the biggest post-order cost for most brands?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        It depends on the category and payment mix. RTO often dominates in COD-heavy businesses, while reverse logistics, failed pickups, support, and inventory losses can cost more in prepaid, return-heavy categories.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">4. What does profit mapping require that standard analytics dashboards do not provide?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Profit mapping requires one shared identifier that connects checkout, courier assignment, delivery outcomes, returns, and refunds. Standard dashboards usually report only the stage managed by their own system.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">5. Why should return reason codes influence courier selection?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Return reasons can reveal lane-specific damage and delivery-quality problems that ordinary logistics metrics miss. Feeding these signals into courier scoring helps brands avoid unsuitable partners for particular pincodes or SKUs.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">6. Does restricting COD to reduce RTO always improve profitability?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        No. Restricting COD can reduce both RTO and conversion, so the net result depends on SKU margin, customer risk, pincode performance, and payment preferences.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">7. How much of return volume is genuine post-purchase dissatisfaction versus checkout-stage failure?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        The split varies by category and payment mix. COD refusals and orders without genuine purchase intent are checkout-stage failures, while product, fit, or quality issues represent post-purchase dissatisfaction.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">8. Can exchange-first nudges hurt profitability?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Yes. Exchanges preserve revenue but introduce reverse pickup, quality checks, inventory reservation, and replacement-shipping costs, so they should be targeted using order value, lane cost, and customer behaviour.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">9. What is the smallest useful version of profit mapping a brand can start with?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Reconstruct every order journey for the five highest-volume SKUs across one month. Include actual shipping, reattempt, reverse-pickup, support, refund, and inventory-outcome costs.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">10. How do delivery delays affect return rates, and is this measurable?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Delivery delays and failed attempts can increase the likelihood of a return after arrival. The effect is measurable when delivery-attempt and elapsed-time data are connected to return outcomes at order level.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="kb-faq-item">
                                    <h4 class="kb-faq-question">11. Which stage of the post-order funnel offers the highest leverage for margin improvement?</h4>
                                    <div class="kb-faq-answer">
                                        Prepaid conversion usually offers strong leverage because it reduces RTO exposure, reattempt risk, and cash-cycle delays together. In prepaid-heavy categories, however, return intelligence and targeted exchange logic may offer more value.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

